fix.shape:
ONE reusable pending-placement arm, modelled on the scale-calibrate round trip but generic, hiding rather than destroying the dialog.

(1) NEW FILE `crates/pdfcer-gui/src/canvas/placement.rs` (~350 lines at this project's doc density). It owns four types and five functions and never sees a dialog type:

```rust
/// What the pointer is being asked to produce. Carried, not one variant per caller —
/// the argument CanvasTool::Markup(kind) makes about mutually exclusive states.
pub enum PlaceShape {
    /// One page point. Click commits; the rect is degenerate.
    Point,
    /// A box of fixed page-space extent whose LOWER-LEFT the pointer sets.
    /// Lower-left, not centre, for canvas/clicking.rs:505's stated reason:
    /// it matches what a drag does, so the two gestures agree.
    FixedBox { w_pt: f64, h_pt: f64 },
    /// A box dragged out, optionally shape-locked.
    DragBox { aspect: Option<f64> },
}

/// Who asked. Opaque to the canvas; handed back verbatim.
#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)]
pub enum PlacementTicket { InsertImage }

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
pub struct PendingPlacement { pub ticket: PlacementTicket, pub page_index: usize, pub shape: PlaceShape }

/// Placed, or abandoned. BOTH are outcomes — an Escape that only disarmed the
/// tool would leave the dialog hidden forever, which is the one way this can
/// strand an operator.
pub enum PlacementOutcome {
    Placed { ticket: PlacementTicket, page_index: usize, rect: pdfcer_core::page_tree::Rect },
    Cancelled { ticket: PlacementTicket },
}

pub fn arm(ctx: &egui::Context, pending: PendingPlacement);   // stores + tool::select(CanvasTool::Place)
pub fn armed(ctx: &egui::Context) -> Option<PendingPlacement>;
pub fn commit(ctx: &egui::Context, rect: Rect);               // called from canvas::clicking
pub fn take_outcome(ctx: &egui::Context) -> Option<PlacementOutcome>; // read-and-clear, measure/mod.rs:523's idiom
pub fn abandon(ctx: &egui::Context) -> bool;                  // Escape's claimant; parks Cancelled
```
All state in `egui::Memory` under a PLACEMENT_MEMORY_KEY, exactly as canvas::measure keeps a half-finished pick, and for its stated reason: a half-finished placement is not part of the document.

(2) `canvas/tool/mod.rs`: one new variant `CanvasTool::Place` — carrying NOTHING, on CanvasTool::Text's own argument (armed or not armed is the whole of what must persist; the shape lives in memory beside the ticket). `cursor()` → Crosshair; `pans_with_primary()` → false. `canvas/tool/arm.rs`: `arm_place`, a `disarm` arm, and an arm in `retire_forbidden`.

(3) `canvas/gesture/meaning.rs`: one rung in `press_kind`, above the Form rung, matching the shape already there — `drag: matches!(shape, DragBox{..}).then_some(DragKind::Place)`, `click: true`. Capability: `edit_content` (an inserted image is page content, not an annotation — the same reasoning that put the Form rung on edit_content).

(4) `canvas/clicking.rs`: one `else if` arm before the Form arm. Converts with the SAME `super::markup::band::endpoints(point, point, page)` the two existing arms use — never a second conversion — builds the rect from `PlaceShape`, and calls `placement::commit`.

(5) `canvas/previews.rs`: one new field `pub place: Option<egui::Rect>` (canvas space). `canvas/painting.rs`: draw it in the last layer beside the measure preview, as an outline only — Rule 4 clean, it is the cursor, it is derived from the rect the click will commit, and it vanishes at commit.

(6) `canvas/keys.rs`: `placement::abandon` joins rung 3a beside `measure::abandon` and `markup::vertex::abandon` — one claimant expressed as another call, on that rung's own 'one tool is armed at a time' argument. It parks `Cancelled` so the dialog comes back.

(7) NEW FILE `crates/pdfcer-gui/src/dialogs/placement.rs` — a second `impl DialogsState` block, because dialogs/mod.rs is at 1,384 lines. It holds `hidden_for_placement: Option<PlacementTicket>`, `fn visible(&self, t: PlacementTicket) -> bool`, `fn take_place_request(&mut self) -> Option<PendingPlacement>` and `fn resume_from_placement(&mut self, outcome: PlacementOutcome)`. `resume_from_placement` is the ONLY per-dialog code: one match arm per opted-in dialog, calling that dialog's `fn placed(&mut self, rect: Rect)`.

(8) `app/frame.rs`: two blocks replacing nothing, sitting beside the existing scale block (which should be left alone; it round-trips a length, not a rect):
```rust
if let Some(pending) = self.dialogs.take_place_request() {
    crate::canvas::placement::arm(&ctx, pending);
}
if let Some(outcome) = crate::canvas::placement::take_outcome(&ctx) {
    self.dialogs.resume_from_placement(outcome);
    crate::canvas::tool::select(&ctx, CanvasTool::Select);
}
```

(9) HOW A DIALOG OPTS IN — three edits, no new machinery:
  a. a `place_requested: bool` field + `take_place_request()` (scale.rs:243's read-and-clear idiom);
  b. a button in the commit row, publishing `crate::diag::ui_rect(REGION_PLACE, r)`;
  c. `fn placed(&mut self, rect: Rect)` seeding the numeric fields from the returned rect — for insert_image, set x_mm/y_mm from rect.llx/lly through the file's existing single `rect_pt` conversion inverted, leaving width_mm/height_mm alone (the operator sized it in the window; the pointer answered only 'where'). Arm with `PlaceShape::FixedBox { w_pt, h_pt }` from the current width/height so the ghost is the real footprint.

(10) BUTTON TEXT, in `src/text/images.rs`, following text/scale.rs:262-280's three-part convention exactly — label, hover saying what happens to the window, small note saying why it is worth pressing:
```rust
pub const fn place_button() -> &'static str { "Place it on the drawing…" }
pub const fn place_tooltip() -> &'static str { "Hide this window and click where the picture goes. It keeps the size set here; the click sets the corner." }
pub const fn place_note() -> &'static str { "Easier than typing coordinates when you can see the spot: point at it, and pdfcer fills the position in." }
```
Use the ellipsis character '…' — scale.rs:263 uses three ASCII dots and is the outlier, worth correcting in the same pass.

R9: the button is unconditionally present whenever the window is open (the window already refuses to open with no page), so nothing is greyed and nothing is a placeholder.
